Q: material-top-tabs label not showing A: <p>With the latest <strong>Tabview</strong> version</p>
<pre><code>&quot;react-native-tab-view&quot;: &quot;^4.2.2&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>We have to use the TabBar like this;</p>
<pre><code>const TabBarStyle = (props: any) =&gt; {
  return (
    &lt;TabBar
      {...props}
      indicatorStyle={styles.indicator}
      style={{ backgroundColor: colors.white, height: 48 }}
      activeColor={colors.primary}
      inactiveColor={colors.gray_59}
    /&gt;
  );
};
</code></pre>
<p>Use <strong>activeColor</strong> and <strong>inactiveColor</strong> for the labels</p>
<p>No need to use this way anymore...</p>
<pre><code>
const TabBarStyle = (props: any) =&gt; {
  return (
    &lt;TabBar
      {...props}
      indicatorStyle={styles.indicator}
      style={{ backgroundColor: colors.white, height: 48 }}
      renderLabel={({ route, focused }: {
        route: Route;
        focused: boolean;
      }) =&gt; (
        &lt;Hstack&gt;
          &lt;AppText
            fontWeight={500}
            style={{
              color: focused ? colors.primary : colors.gray_59,
              fontSize: 18,
              textAlign: 'center',
            }}&gt;
            {route.title}
          &lt;/AppText&gt;
        &lt;/Hstack&gt;
      )}
    /&gt;
  );
};
</code></pre>
<p><strong>renderLabel</strong> will not work.</p>
<p>Hope this will help anyone....</p>

Q: How to align adjacent cells to the top when one cell has dynamic multi-line text? A: <p>Set <strong>vertical alignment to top</strong> on the cells.</p>
<h4>CSS example</h4>
<pre><code>td, th {
  vertical-align: top;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This ensures all adjacent cells align to the top, even when one cell grows due to wrapped or multi-line content.</p>
<hr />
<h3>If you are using a specific layout</h3>
<h4>HTML table</h4>
<pre><code>&lt;td style=&quot;vertical-align: top;&quot;&gt;
  Content
&lt;/td&gt;
</code></pre>
<h4>Flexbox (common mistake)</h4>
<p>Flexbox does <strong>not</strong> use <code>vertical-align</code>.</p>
<p>Use:</p>
<pre><code>.container {
  align-items: flex-start;
}
</code></pre>
<h4>CSS Grid</h4>
<pre><code>.container {
  align-items: start;
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h3>Why this happens</h3>
<p>When one cell expands because of wrapped text, other cells keep their default vertical alignment (usually middle). Explicitly setting top alignment keeps everything consistent.</p>

Q: &lt;canvas&gt; drawing slows down 200000x at regular intervals A: <p><em>Disclaimer: I didn't check in the sources that it's actually what happens, so take it as an educated guess.</em></p>
<p>In general (at least for GPU accelerated 2D canvas) the draw calls are actually coalesced, so that the CPU can pass all the drawing operations in a single call to the GPU.<br />
Even though you are on a deaccelerated system, it's very possible you are facing a similar situation. And the fact that you face a fixed number of iteration would mean they have a fixed size storage for these draw calls before they move it to the renderer.<br />
Since your outer loops are ran synchronously, only a few of these will actually measure the time taken by the renderer. The other ones are just plain copying of method calls and params.</p>
<p>We can see that by adding a simple <code>await new Promise(res =&gt; setTimeout(res))</code> between each outer loop. Now the timings seem consistent.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d', {
  willReadFrequently: true, // disable Hardware Acceleration
});
ctx.clearRect(0, 0, 1, 1); // warmup
function drawLine() {
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.moveTo(10, 10);
  ctx.lineTo(190, 190);
  ctx.stroke();
}

async function runBenchmark() {
  const runAsync = asyncInput.checked;
  const innerLoops = +innerInput.value || 10000;
  const outerLoops = +outerInput.value || 10;

  const times = [];

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; outerLoops; i++) {
    const start = performance.now();

    for (let j = 0; j &lt; innerLoops; j++) {
      drawLine();
    }

    times.push(performance.now() - start);
    // Don't run the tests synchronously
    if (runAsync) await new Promise(res =&gt; setTimeout(res));
  }

  results.innerHTML = times.map((t, i) =&gt; `Run ${i+1}: ${t.toFixed(2)} ms`).join('&lt;br&gt;');
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;canvas id="canvas" height="80"&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;Run async&lt;input type="checkbox" id="asyncInput" checked&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;Inner loops: &lt;input type="number" id="innerInput" value="10000"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;Outer loops: &lt;input type="number" id="outerInput" value="30"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;button onclick="runBenchmark()"&gt;Run Benchmark&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div id="results"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How do I bind a SwiftUI element to a value in a Dictionary? A: <p>I would do the following, in case you don't mind the force cast:</p>
<pre><code>extension Binding {
    /// Creates a binding to the value for the given key in the dictionary.
    func key&lt;K, T&gt;(_ key: K) -&gt; Binding&lt;T&gt; where Value == Dictionary&lt;K, T&gt; {
        .init(
            get: {
                self.wrappedValue[key]!
            },
            set: {
                self.wrappedValue[key]! = $0
            }
        )
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Usage: <code>$dict.key(&quot;myExistingKey&quot;)</code></p>
<p>----------------------</p>
<p>If you care about possibly non-existent keys:</p>
<pre><code>extension Binding {
    /// Creates a binding to the value for the given key in the dictionary.
    func key&lt;K, T&gt;(_ key: K) -&gt; Binding&lt;T?&gt; where Value == Dictionary&lt;K, T&gt; {
        .init(
            get: {
                self.wrappedValue[key]
            },
            set: {
                self.wrappedValue[key] = $0
            }
        )
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Usage: <code>$dict.key(&quot;myOptionalKey&quot;)</code></p>
<p>----------------------</p>
<p>If you want to have the default subscript behavior of dictionaries, you can create a <code>subscript</code> over a function:</p>
<pre><code>extension Binding {
    /// Creates a binding to the value for the given key in the dictionary.
    subscript&lt;K, T&gt;(_ key: K) -&gt; Binding&lt;T?&gt; where Value == Dictionary&lt;K, T&gt; {
        .init(
            get: {
                self.wrappedValue[key]
            },
            set: {
                self.wrappedValue[key] = $0
            }
        )
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Usage: <code>$dict[&quot;myOptionalKey&quot;]</code></p>
